# Day 4 — Assembly Testing and Retention Redesign

The fourth stage focused on testing how the retaining rail interacted with the enclosure and PCB.

A SolidWorks assembly was created so the railing could be positioned and rotated around the pivot.

### Initial Assembly

- Inserted the metronome casing and retaining rail into a SolidWorks assembly
- Used a **Concentric Mate** to align the M3 pivot holes
- Used a **Coincident Mate** to position the rail against the rear surface of the enclosure
- Left rail rotation unlocked so the retaining bar could rotate around the screw

![Day 4 - Initial assembly](images/day4-09.png)

### Initial Rail Test

The original design used a single retaining rail across the PCB.

Testing the assembly showed that this design would obstruct access to buttons and other components located near the center of the PCB.

![Day 4 - Original retaining rail](images/day4-10.png)

### Retention System Redesign

The retaining system was redesigned so that the middle of the PCB remained accessible.

- Designed another retaining rail
- Secondary rail dimensions:
  - **110 mm long**
  - **12 mm wide**
  - **4 mm thick**
- Added another M3-compatible pivot
- Repositioned the retaining rails so that they hold the PCB along its **top and bottom edges**
- Kept the center of the PCB clear for access to controls and electrical components

![Day 4 - Revised retaining rail](images/day4-11.png)

![Day 4 - Two-rail assembly](images/day4-12.png)

![Day 4 - Final retaining rail positions](images/day4-13.png)

### Rail Movement

The retaining rails were intentionally left rotationally unlocked in the assembly.

This allows them to swing away from the PCB when the board needs to be installed or removed.

[Watch the retaining rail motion test](images/railing-motion.mp4)

